API keys now have a scope, either `general` (full access, as before) or `lightroom` (create-only). Go tests, `npm run typecheck`, the frontend tests and the SQL-layer check all pass. I did not run the full `scripts/check.sh` gate, and I did not commit, as instructed.

A lightroom key can only `POST` these four routes:
- `/api/posts`
- `/api/tags`
- `/api/media/upload`
- `/api/media/upload/multiple`

Every other route answers 403. On public reads it sees the guest view instead of the owner's hidden items, and it is refused on the MCP endpoint.

The scope is stored in a new `scope` column on `api_keys`, which defaults to `general`, so existing keys keep full access. The `/api/auth/api-keys` endpoint takes an optional `scope` and rejects unknown values. The admin UI has a scope column and a "Create Lightroom Key" button. I added a short note to `docs/features/auth.md`.

Lightroom keys cannot read anything with the key, not even tags, so a client that must look up an existing tag first would need a general key. That follows the bead's "create only" wording. If you want read access for tags, it is a small change to the allow-list in `lightroomAllowed` (`api/internal/api/middleware.go`).

I added a middleware test for the allow-list and a service test for scope validation. The bead is closed and `progress.md` is updated.

